body:prose = >`
  &ND v2

  # AEON Profile-Owned Generic Admission Candidate [# profile-owned-generic-admission]

  This proposal governs the mutable conformance targets for generic admission.
  Released Core, profile, and CTS contracts keep their original expectations.

  ## Core descriptor preservation [# core-descriptors]

  A datatype descriptor in any legal annotation position may carry ordered,
  nonempty type-descriptor arguments and a clarifier list. Nested descriptors
  may carry both. Core enforces descriptor syntax, datatype and literal-family
  compatibility, mode and datatype policy, and configured resource limits.
  Literal values cannot serve as generic arguments. In custom mode
  [$ r:rational<number> = |1/3|] preserves the custom claim and symbolic value.
  Source canonicalization and AES preserve the descriptor and value family.
  A generic claim does not by itself convert or materialize the payload.

  ## General-purpose profile admission [# gp-admission]

  Trusted selection applies the GP rules below. A document profile declaration
  can serve as a routing hint. This candidate records the generic policy in a
  separate record from the existing clarifier policy.

  The candidate profile record is
  [$ resources/contracts/candidates/gp-generic-admission.aeon].

  The reserved list is closed. Custom labels may carry arguments when admitted
  by the effective datatype policy. A custom annotation with arguments on an
  assigned value requires a list or tuple, including when the value is reached
  through a reference. A node head admits arguments only on [$ node]. A
  directly applied binding-side [$ node] descriptor admits [$ node] or custom
  arguments. Evaluate a nested descriptor in its argument position. The
  binding-side condition does not apply to [$ list<node<radix\\[2\\]>>].
  GP continues to reject explicit clarifiers on node heads and on [$ symbol].

  In strict mode [$ duration:symbol<time> = |1min|] preserves the symbolic
  value. The argument [$ time] names a reserved datatype. Its presence does
  not turn the value into a time literal or assert a duration interpretation.

  ## Permissive test profile [# permissive-test-profile]

  [$ set.to.eleven.v1] applies no generic or clarifier admission policy.
  Core syntax, literal-family compatibility and resource limits still apply.
  One test case is [$ duration:symbol<time>\\["minutes"\\] = |1min|].

  AEOS argument-count constraints and binary compatibility qualification are
  separate work on the proposal.
`
